# Production environment for the man-hour management app.
#
# Copy to .env.production and edit before the first `docker compose up`:
#     cp .env.production.example .env.production
#
# .env.production is gitignored. This example file is the only one tracked.
# Never put a real credential in this file.
#
# These are ALL the environment variables the application code reads. Verified
# by searching the source: DATABASE_URL, ORG_DATA_SOURCE, ADMIN_PASSWORD,
# SESSION_SECRET, COOKIE_SECURE, NODE_ENV. Nothing else has any effect, so do
# not expect to configure ports, thresholds, or feature flags here - none exist
# yet (see D-212, deferred to v2).
# NODE_ENV and TZ are set by docker-compose.prod.yml, not here.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DATABASE_URL - Prisma datasource (required)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MUST BE AN ABSOLUTE PATH. This is not a style preference.
#
# The standalone server calls process.chdir(__dirname) at startup, so a relative
# path like "file:./dev.db" resolves against the server's own directory instead
# of where you expect. better-sqlite3 then CREATES a new 0-byte file there
# rather than failing, Prisma raises P2021 (table does not exist), and
# src/lib/org-source.ts classifies that as an infrastructure error and falls
# back to demo data - while still returning HTTP 200. The result is a dashboard
# full of fabricated numbers that looks completely healthy. This was reproduced,
# not theorised.
#
# /app/data is the container path of the ./data bind mount defined in
# docker-compose.prod.yml. The value below is correct for that setup as written
# and normally needs no editing.
DATABASE_URL="file:/app/data/dev.db"

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ORG_DATA_SOURCE - dashboard data source (required in production)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# "db"   = read real data from the database.
# "mock" = serve the Phase 1 in-memory fixtures.
#
# MUST be "db" in production. With "mock" the dashboard renders plausible but
# entirely fictional hours, and the only on-screen indication is the 演示数据
# marker on the home page. Server-only: deliberately NOT prefixed with
# NEXT_PUBLIC_, so it is never exposed to the browser bundle.
ORG_DATA_SOURCE=db

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ADMIN_PASSWORD - shared administrator password (required)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 看板 (/) is public and needs nothing. Everything else - 实绩, 计划录入, 管理, and
# all ten write actions - requires this password once, then holds a signed cookie
# for 30 days.
#
# Leave it empty and the app fails CLOSED: the login page states that no password
# is configured and nobody, including administrators, can write anything. That is
# deliberate. A misconfigured deployment must lock the operator out, never let the
# public in.
#
# There is one password for all administrators, so the audit trail can answer
# "what changed and when" but not "who changed it" (D-143). Pick something the
# one or two people who need it will not write on a sticky note.
ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SESSION_SECRET - cookie signing key (required, >= 32 chars)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The session cookie is `<timestamp>.<HMAC-SHA256>`. This is the HMAC key. Without
# it - or with fewer than 32 characters - the app denies everyone rather than
# admitting everyone.
#
# Generate a fresh one per deployment, never reuse the dev value:
#     node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
#
# Changing it invalidates every existing session; administrators just log in again.
SESSION_SECRET=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# COOKIE_SECURE - mark the session cookie `secure` (optional, default false)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MUST stay false while nginx.conf listens on port 80 only. A `secure` cookie is
# never sent back over plain HTTP, so setting this true without TLS locks every
# administrator out with no error message anywhere - the login appears to succeed
# and the next page asks for the password again.
#
# Set true only after the deployment actually terminates TLS. Until then the
# accepted tradeoff is that both the password and the cookie are visible to anyone
# capturing intranet traffic.
COOKIE_SECURE=false
